  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A tiger 虍 standing on a rock 几; 虍 also provides the pronunciation"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a tiger."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "山獸之君。从虍，虎足象人足。象形。凡虎之屬皆从虎。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形). 虍 represents the tiger's head. The torso has disappeared and the legs and the tail have transformed into 人 in the small seal script and later 儿 in the clerical script.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *k-la (“tiger”), from Proto-Austroasiatic *kla(ː)ʔ (“tiger”). Cognate with 菟 (OC *daː) in 於菟 (OC *qa daː, “tiger”). Compare Vietnamese khái (\"tiger\").\nHill (2019) compares it to Tibetan སྟག (stag, “tiger”); however, Zheng Zining deems that comparison \"probably spurious\"."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "山獸之君。从虍，虎足象人足。象形。凡虎之屬皆从虎。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "sovereign of mountain; beast. Pictograph depicting the form of man foot 。 elephant. All characters in the 虎 \"tiger\" category use this as radical.",
